re's  still a centaur there as the object  of my hallucination so uh  as far as who's concerned consciousness  is pointed at objects whether we like it  or not  and so there's no point in wondering how  we leap from outside of our mind into a  world with objects  heidegger's criticism of the realist  question is similar  heidegger thinks that humans are always  already thrown into the world  and we are counting on and relying on  practical devices  we are we are not primarily looking at  the world i'm primarily right now using  the chair  using the atmospheric oxygen in the room  to stay alive by breathing using the  smooth function of my bodily organs to  stay alive  and so forth so for both of them we're  always  already involved with things with the  world and therefore there's no  point in coming up with complex  epistemological maneuvers to try to  prove that we can get out of the mind  into another world  and so for this reason realism was never  taken  all that seriously in the continental  tradition with the exception of nikolai  hartmann the german philosopher whose  the rough contemporary of heidegger is  experiencing a revival  these days but he was so marginalized  compared to russo and heidegger who  really set the tone  and so if you had written a dissertation  in the continental philosophy department  25 years ago or 20 years ago when i was  doing it  uh on the existence of the external  worlds people would be smirking and  chuckling behind your back because it  wasn't considered a serious problem  this is the four of us at 2007 in  goldsmiths  so within this context uh speculative  realism  uh tried to argue for realism within a  continental  context would have been not news at all  in the analytic philosophy context where  realism  has always been a very robust presence  but in the continental uh tradition this  was  uh fairly controversial and i think it's  had an effect you  you hear more people now offering  realist interpretations of philosophers  such as varied on  and heidegger who wouldn't have been  associated with the world  word realism at all and i think a lot of  that is people responding to what the  speculative realist did  i want to say a couple of other things  about this slide before we get going  uh the first is that you see four white  guys here  and this is an era when in philosophy in  particular we're especially concerned  with diversity and  inclusion so i sometimes have asked why  was it for white guys  well the simplest answer to that is that  it was four people who knew  loosely knew each other brassiere knew  on the right new ian hamilton grant on  the left  and there there are my second from the  left i knew contact  somewhat from having met him in paris  and so ray brasia and i simply brought  together  each of us brought together one  acquaintance we knew with similar  philosophical interests  and we combined for this event the other  thing is you have to remember this was  not a very prestigious group at the time  all four of us were quite marginalized  in our profession 13 years ago  not very visible we were starting to  publish  uh but did not have especially  prestigious  academic positions or or acclaim at that  time and so it was a  meeting of the marginalized and so if  anyone else had wanted to join us at the  time  of any background we would have been  more than delighted so that's that's my  answer to that question  now there's another question which has  to do with um  the basis of this philosophical movement  usually when you see  a group of philosophers come together  there's some shared tradition or some  shared inspiring figure who they're all  working out of so you see heideggerians  inspired by heidegger delusions inspired  by zulus  and so forth what was interesting about  this group  is that we don't really have any shared  philosophical heroes there's no  philosopher that all four of us were  passionate about  in fact in many cases the other members  disliked s