cier  is  probably arguably the most pessimistic  philosopher in the western tradition  since schopenhauer and edward van harman  uh rossier thinks he stresses not only  that the universe will become extinct  someday  he says that since the universe will  become extinct someday you're already  dead  you may think you're alive but you're  already dead  um nihilism needle unbound is the title  of his  his book and uh he thinks that  science and science-based philosophy are  speculative tools  that enhance our incentives to be  nihilists  that there is a gap between any  scientific theory and reality and  irremedial  irremediable gap and therefore what  science is really doing is it's not  trying to teach us positive truths about  the worlds because though  each of those scientific theories lasts  only for a little while before it's  shown to be inadequate  that scientific theories are there to  show us the  ultimate nullity of each of our theories  about the cosmos and it's a progressive  process we're becoming less and less  attached to this world  as we uh become acquainted with the  nothingness of it  who was then a tutor at the economy  superior since then has been called  to a professorship at the sorbonne  executive realism made him a lot more  visible  i've written an entire book on maya sue  whose philosophy fascinates me  maesu is a realist in the sense that he  he starts with a conflict of what he  calls an apparea between  two different truths on the one hand  he says that it's true that  anything we say about the world is  simply us saying it  anything we say about the world is  correlated with us he calls this  correlationism  there's this idea that if i say  something about the world it's my saying  the thing about the world it's not  uh me standing in a vacuum initially  stating true propositions about the  world i'm trapped in a correlate  between myself and the world and so  statement about the world  is a statement about the world as given  to me  i 